  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 121 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nLate this weekend into early next week, split upper-level flow will \ncontinue across North America as an amplified ridge remains \nestablished across the northern Plains. This pattern will favor the \npolar jet stream remaining displaced north of the forecast area, \nwhile the southern-stream jet remains farther south across the \nsouthern CONUS. With the strongest forcing for ascent remaining \ndisplaced north and south of the region, generally dry conditions \nare expected across western and north-central Nebraska. A shortwave \ntrough may track southeastward across the northern Plains along the \nnorthern periphery of the ridge, though current guidance suggests \nlimited impacts across the forecast area. Persistent ridging, along \nwith anomalously warm temperatures in the lower to middle levels, \nwill support well above-normal temperatures through the early \nportion of next week. Highs Sunday and Monday should reach the \nmiddle to upper 60s across much of the area, with overnight lows \ngenerally in the middle to upper 30s.\n\nTuesday into the middle of next week, another shortwave trough is \nexpected to track southeastward across the northern Plains, \nresulting in a transition toward more northwesterly flow aloft \nacross the region. An associated cold front may move through the \nforecast area, bringing a modest decrease in temperatures. However, \nwith the deeper reservoir of cold air remaining displaced well north \nof the region, significant cooling is not anticipated. Highs should \ngenerally remain in the upper 50s to lower 60s, which would still be \nabove normal for early November. Beyond Tuesday, upper-level ridging \nis expected to remain persistent across the central United States \nthrough the end of the forecast period. The broader upper-level \npattern may take on characteristics of an omega block, with \ntroughing across the eastern Pacific and northeastern United States \nsurrounding a ridge across the central CONUS. This pattern would \nfavor continued subsidence and limited precipitation chances across \nwestern and north-central Nebraska, while maintaining above-normal \ntemperatures. Highs should generally remain in the lower 60s through \nthe latter portion of next week. Additionally, persistent troughing \nnear the Aleutians and Gulf of Alaska may continue to favor the \ntransport of relatively mild Pacific air into portions of the CONUS. \nWith the primary reservoir of Arctic air remaining displaced \nnorthward into Canada and no strong signal for a significant \nsouthward intrusion of colder air, temperatures are expected to \nremain above normal through the end of the period. While some \nfluctuations in temperatures are possible with passing shortwaves \nand associated frontal boundaries, the overall pattern continues to \nfavor warmer and drier conditions across the Central Plains.",
